"You are a helpful AI assistant that answers questions based on the provided context.\n\nTOPIC OVERVIEW:\nPOSITIVE TOPICS:\n- Students discuss how to fit co-op work experiences into their degree plans and how to handle the timing and uncertainty of job and return offers.\n- Discussions about social life and campus culture at a co-op-oriented university, covering the challenge of making friends amid rotating co-op schedules, the career-driven character of the student body, and the availability and erosion of free student perks.\n- Students discuss the challenges of finding healthcare, hospital, and biotech co-op placements, especially with limited experience, and share strategies for breaking into these competitive fields.\n\nNEUTRAL TOPICS:\n- Discussions about a university's cooperative education program, focusing on international students' concerns over co-op availability, competitiveness, visa limits, and return on tuition cost, alongside commemorative posts honoring a former student whose co-op success symbolized her promise.\n- Students ask whether co-op start dates can be pushed back to fit around other commitments such as summer internships, and responses say flexibility depends on employer size, candidate strength, and the hiring channel.\n\nNEGATIVE TOPICS:\n- Students discuss how co-op programs affect course scheduling, reduced-courseload petitions, graduation timelines, financial aid coverage, and work authorization for international students, often seeking guidance from advisors and administrative offices.\n- Students discuss how hard it is to land co-op placements in a tough job market, including heavy application volume with few interviews, strategies for improving their chances, the mental health toll of the search, and disappointment with unfulfilling co-op roles.\n\n    \nCONTEXT:\n[{'id': '1xhblex', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1xhblex: Housing Waiver\\n\\nI’m currently a first year student, and I want to live off campus next semester(Spring 2027)\\n\\nWhat is the best reason to apply residency waiver request? I’m not sure if allergy can be a good reason.\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment 19rry6tahhyw0: Genuinely, all you need is a signed lease. That’s all I needed to get mine approved.\\n    - Comment 19rtcoh98myg5: Thank you very much, that’s really helpful!\\n- Comment 19rrcfvf1d143: Sick family member you need to take care of.\\n    - Comment 19rtcqryluxuq: I’m an international student, so take care of family members might not work for me🥲'}, {'id': '1x6w217', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1x6w217: International Student: CPT Guidance on Co-op (Undergrad). Is Optional Co-op still allowed?\\n\\nSay it’s NOT required to get a co-op to graduate, will I still be allowed to be on co-op anymore?\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment ph1f5u2: OGS is the only place you should be seeking information about anything that relates to international student status. [\\n- Comment ph0bl18: According to new rules most likely no, but ask your advisor not Reddit'}, {'id': '1x0jd5l', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1x0jd5l: Accepted for CPS, NEU\\n\\nHey, Husky's\\n\\nI got admission into CPS, NEU Boston campus for Spring intake. However, I haven't received the I-20 yet, I have raised a request in my graduation application portal but theres no response yet. Could someone please help me how to get the I-20? Should I submit my financial ability to OGS in order to get I-20? \\n\\nAlso, regarding the stay. I request some help about the places/residential places to stay. Kindly, reach me out directly in dm or reply to this thread. Your help will be much appreciated.\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment penjgnb: Per Claude:\\n\\n**1. Be admitted and confirm enrollment**  \\nAfter admission, the student must **accept/confirm enrollment** and complete the required items in Northeastern’s admissions portal. Northeastern generally requires any enrollment deposit to be paid before the I-20 request becomes available.   \\n**2. Request the I-20 through myOGS**  \\nOnce eligible, the student accesses **myOGS** and submits the electronic **Initial I-20 Request**.  \\nThe student generally uploads:  \\nPassport identification page  \\nProof of sufficient financial resources for the program  \\nCurrency conversion if financial documents aren’t in U.S. dollars  \\nSponsor letter/affidavit if the sponsor is a non-blood relative  \\nThe financial documentation must demonstrate funds sufficient for Northeastern’s estimated tuition/fees and living expenses.   \\nNortheastern’s current pre-arrival materials say **initial I-20 processing is approximately 15 business days**, although it can fluctuate during busy periods.   \\n**3. Northeastern issues the I-20**  \\nThe I-20 is the university-issued **Certificate of Eligibility for F-1 status**. It contains the student’s SEVIS ID and program information.  \\n**The I-20 is not the F-1 visa.** The sequence is:  \\n**Northeastern admission → I-20 → SEVIS fee → DS-160 → visa interview → F-1 visa → travel to U.S.**  \\nNortheastern specifically lists this sequence on its current F-1 visa page.   \\n**4. Pay the I-901 SEVIS fee**  \\nAfter receiving the I-20, the student generally pays the **I-901 SEVIS fee**. A SEVIS transfer student generally doesn’t pay it again.   \\n**5. Complete the DS-160**  \\nThe student completes the U.S. Department of State’s **DS-160 Online Nonimmigrant Visa Application** and pays the applicable visa application fee.  \\nNortheastern says the student should have the following available:  \\nI-20  \\nPassport  \\nSEVIS I-901 receipt  \\nNortheastern admission/acceptance letter  \\nFinancial documentation  \\nDS-160 confirmation  \\nRequired photograph   \\n**6. Schedule the F-1 visa interview**  \\nThe student schedules an appointment with a U.S. embassy/consulate.  \\n**Important current rule:** Northeastern’s September 2026 guidance says that, since September 6, 2025, F/J visa applicants generally must schedule their interview in their **country of nationality or residence**.   \\n**7. Attend the interview**  \\nThe consular officer decides whether to issue the F-1 visa. The student should be prepared to explain:  \\nWhy Northeastern  \\nWhat program they will study  \\nHow the education will be financed  \\nTheir academic background  \\nTheir plans after completing their studies  \\nAdditional documentation can be requested, including academic records and evidence relevant to the student’s intent to depart the U.S. after the authorized period of study.   \\n**8. Receive the F-1 visa and travel**  \\nOnce approved, the F-1 visa is placed in the passport.  \\nNortheastern says F-1 students can apply for the visa **up to 120 days before the I-20 program start date**.   \\nThe student then travels to the U.S. with the required immigration documents, including the passport with F-1 visa and I-20.  \\n**9. Complete Northeastern’s immigration clearance after arrival**  \\nAfter arriving in the U.S., the student must complete Northeastern OGS’s **Online Immigration Clearance**. For a new student with an initial I-20, Northeastern currently requires this within **30 days of the program start date**. The student provides the passport, F-1 visa, I-94 and U.S. address.\\n    - Comment penkzyb: Thank you very much!!\\n- Comment penktpx: For CPS Graduate programs, they list the following for financial requirements \\n\\n**I-20 calculation**  \\n**Amount**  \\nTuition & fees  \\n**$24,192**  \\nLiving expenses  \\n**$27,728**  \\n**Total funds required**  \\n**$51,920**\"}, {'id': '1wo77dg', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1wo77dg: Anyone else received an OGS warning about taking an online certificate while on OPT?\\n\\nI’m on F-1 OPT/cap-gap, and my H-1B starts October 1. I’ve been taking a part-time online graduate certificate at Northeastern for about 1.5 years. Before enrolling, I spoke with OGS and my immigration attorney, and I was told it shouldn’t be an issue since it’s online, non-degree, and doesn’t provide F-1 status.\\n\\nYesterday, OGS suddenly told me that it could affect my OPT and asked me to withdraw from the program and both courses.\\n\\nHas anyone dealt with something similar? What happened after you withdrew? Did it affect your OPT, cap-gap, or H-1B?\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment pblbcxs: The CPT guidelines are a moving target.  Check with OGS and don’t rely on information  posted here, things are changing by the hour.'}, {'id': '1wgneah', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1wgneah: Transfer question\\n\\n\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment paabafy: I did. Youll just have to take it later on .Dw too much about it'}, {'id': '1wgjl00', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1wgjl00: Clinical Research co-ops Question\\n\\nI understand that Patient Care clinical co-ops are historically released after Indigenous Peoples' Day. However, I was wondering when CRC or CRA positions are released during that time frame as well. \\n\\nCurrently, there are only a handful of them on Nuworks, and I was curious as to whether there will be more posted or if they will be posted with the PCA positions during October.\\n\\nThis will be my second co-op, for context.\\n\\nThanks in advance\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment pbdn7s9: They will be posted with PCA\"}, {'id': '1wbl1n6', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1wbl1n6: CPT rule change????\\n\\nI recently got a fall co-op and I'm supposed to start next week (haven't received CPT yet) but I received the email about CPT changes yesterday and now apparently can't do the co-op. All my employer documents and contracts are signed. What am I supposed to do now?\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment p8r59iq: Whats that rule change I didnt get any email or announcements\\n    - Comment p8siwiw: You can only get authorized for the co-ops that are strictly required, meaning only 1 for most programs\\n- Comment p9g5c4w: I just got rejected a 2nd cpt yesterday\\n    - Comment p9w6cub: I'm sorry, is there any action you're taking?\\n- Comment p8rfd4s: You can still do coop just no more than 1\"}, {'id': '1vb8bn9', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1vb8bn9: Can London Scholars students do co op in the second semester of sophomore year?\\n\\nI will be a Northeastern London Scholars student. I’ll spend my first year in London, then move to the Boston campus for my second year on an F-1 visa.\\n\\nSome Northeastern students can start co op in the spring semester of their second year. Would I also be eligible to do that, or would the F-1 CPT one academic year requirement mean I have to wait until fall of my third year?\\n\\nHas anyone in London Scholars been able to start co-op during spring of sophomore year?\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment p0sfwr2: depends what co-op cycle you are assigned to.\\n    - Comment p4xu96z: How and when will I know?'}, {'id': '1vnplvc', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1vnplvc: How will this DHS memo affect co-op at Northeastern?\\n\\nThis is a UC Berkeley International Office (BIO) notice dated August 13, 2026, informing students that an ICE guidance memo received August 12 narrows CPT authorization to cases where the internship is required for all students to earn the degree. As a result, BIO is temporarily suspending all \"Course Credit CPT\" applications (integral to the program but not a degree requirement) while continuing to process \"Degree-Requirement\" CPT as normal. The office says it is consulting legal counsel and notes possible legal challenges that could allow course-credit CPT processing to resume.\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment p4i6too: International students you will now require a breathing license, approved by DHS.\\n- Comment p3jcq49: ETA: for international students who require CPT authorization to work off campus.\\n\\nHere’s the memo from ICE to DSOs across the US:\\n- Comment p3kl4qh: Northeastern’s current OGS CPT policy already describes CPT as experiential learning that is “necessary and integral, or required” as part of an academic program. It specifically says CPT authorization is required for every co-op/internship/etc.  \\n\\nAnd Northeastern explicitly says international students can participate in co-op with CPT authorization.  \\n\\nSo, at least on the face of the current Northeastern policy:\\n\\nICE’s new memo doesn’t contradict Northeastern’s co-op system.  \\nGot it from ChatGPT'}, {'id': '1wwd4sp', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1wwd4sp: [General Question] Commerce → MSCS: Has anyone with a similar non-CS background received F-1 approval? What questions did the visa officer ask about the academic/career transition and CS preparation? Any 2025–26 Indian student experiences would be greatly appreciated.\\n\\n\\n\\n## Comments:'}, {'id': '1xf5vnm', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1xf5vnm: Graduate Admissions\\n\\nAs per the link - [  \\nMinimum CGPA requirements for admission state 8.0 on 10.0 scale. Is this a new requirement for spring / fall 2027 admissions?\\n\\n## Comments:'}, {'id': '1w6pdno', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1w6pdno: Boston federal judge blasts Trump administration’s changes to international student visas\\n\\nFound this very interesting synopsis of arguments in federal court and the dismal reception the Trump administration's arguments were received by the federal judge. This should be of interest to all of our international Huskies\\n\\n## Comments:\"}, {'id': '1wml31d', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1wml31d: Missing classes for neu london scholars\\n\\n**Matched keywords**: visa\\n**Found in**: post, comment\\n\\nso they told us that skipping classes and getting bellow i think 70% attendance means that your visa will be removed. I am currently kinda sick but am too scared to miss classes. Have anyone else had experience with missing classes as a london cholar? they say to put evidence in the seats app but what do i put for a mild cough and cold? also if i miss a class what happens? do i get a notification or what?\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- **Matched Comment pbiaeqs**: Was at London last year, do not worry. You can miss 2 classes a week and your attendance will still be 75% for the week. If you have below 70% for 2 weeks IN A ROW then you will get a warning and then if u have 4 weeks in A ROW then you will get another warning and a meeting, and then 6 weeks in a row is another meeting and the last warning and then 8 weeks IN A ROW is visa removal. So basically you can miss 2 classes a week for the whole year and nothing will happen. Basically impossible to get your visa revoked unless you miss 3 classes a week for 8 weeks. Just stick to missing 2 a week and maybe you can skip 3 one week and then only miss 1 class the next week and you will be at 70%\\n- Comment pbjsokj: Thank you!\\n- Comment pbjsrha: What did you put for request in absense in seats tho? And did you email your professors everytime?'}, {'id': '1w7iz8d', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1w7iz8d: flight is monday and still no visa decision, anyone else dealt with this?\\n\\n**Matched keywords**: visa\\n**Found in**: post, comment\\n\\nso my flight to london is this monday and i still havent gotten a decision on my uk student visa. did biometrics july 29 so its already way past the standard 3 week processing time. got the \"taking longer than usual\" email about 2 weeks ago saying they don\\'t need anything else from me. submitted an enquiry and they told me it\\'s \"under active consideration\" and awaiting a decision, nothing needed from me unless they reach out. tried calling but couldn\\'t get through.\\n\\ndoes ukvi ever give decisions on weekends or is it strictly mon-fri? trying to figure out if theres any chance i hear back before monday or if im basically out of luck. anyone been in a similar spot and it worked out ok?\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- **Matched Comment p7vhnmx**: Heh, hello fellow Husky. Sorry to hear that you’re still having issues with this. Have you talked to OGS? Is London campus on same schedule as Boston with classes starting Wednesday? Maybe do reach out to Professors and start remotely? I had a class last fall with an International student who started in person 2 weeks late because of Donnie Trump administration issues with getting his visa\\n- **Matched Comment p7vm4w4**: Thanks for the help, yes i contacted OGS they said they cant really do anythig but they will help me if i get my visa later! No i think for us the calsses start on 14th september i was more so bummed out i would not be able to do the welcome week activities and that it may be harder to make friends since i wont be there for welcome week\\n- Comment p85r6t2: That’s a sign to skip the school and the loan burden\\n- **Matched Comment pakg50q**: OP, how did things go? You get your Visa and in London now?\\n- **Matched Comment pakowwk**: Got the visa literally 10 hours before my flight and made it in time! a fellow redditor really helped out. If enouogh people ask ill write a whole thing about EVERYTHING i did cause it was a lot lol'}, {'id': '1w4sepz', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1w4sepz: My visa is not here and I leave for London in 6 days!\\n\\n**Matched keywords**: visa\\n**Found in**: post, comment\\n\\nSo as the title suggests my flight for London is in 6 days so this Monday. And I still haven’t received my London visa. I took the biometric appointment on 29th July so it’s been way more than 3weeks. They did email me saying that the visa is taking time and they don’t need anything from me about 2 weeks ago. I’m just stressed and wondering if I’ll get it in time. I was wondering if anyone else has had a similar experience.\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- **Matched Comment p7d8gji**: UK can be very quick in visa processing. You might get it by tomorrow if it's approved today. I heard there is an expedited option too in case you want to try it.\\n- Comment p7f3ihh: see the dm I just sent you\\n- Comment p79xyxd: Have you considered going in person to the British consulate?\\n- Comment p7ame2i: I don’t think the consulate would be able to do anything since ukvi makes the decision no?\"}, {'id': '1xxe8u2', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1xxe8u2: Does co-op actually make Northeastern affordable for international student ?\\n\\n**Matched keywords**: visa, CPT\\n**Found in**: post\\n\\nHi everyone,\\n\\n\\n\\nI’m an international student looking at Northeastern for MS in Computer Science , and the co-op program is a big part of why it’s on my list. I’d really like to hear from people who have been through it, especially other international students.\\n\\n\\n\\nFrom the outside, co-op looks like a great way to gain real work experience while offsetting the cost of tuition, but I want to understand how it plays out in practice. A few things I’m curious about:\\n\\n\\n\\nHow much of your tuition did co-op earnings realistically cover? Did it make a noticeable difference, or was it more of a “helps with living expenses” situation?\\n\\nHow hard was it to land a paid co-op as an international student? Did visa/CPT constraints limit your options?\\n\\nWere there any hidden costs or surprises (tuition during co-op semesters, fees, relocation, timing issues, etc.)?\\n\\nWas the co-op pay enough to live on, especially in expensive cities like Boston or the Bay Area?\\n\\nWould you still choose Northeastern for the co-op if you were making the decision again, given the total cost?\\n\\n\\n\\nI’m not looking for a sales pitch in either direction. Honest experiences, good or bad, would help a lot. If you’re willing to share rough numbers or your timeline, even better.\\n\\n\\n\\nThanks in advance!\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment 19spb8pwsww8w: Nothing is affordable about studying in northeastern without a grant/scholarship as an international student. Coop makes some money sure but you’re at a temporary entry level position.\\n- Comment 19spd0kxc30w2: I agree with this. Are you trying to compare it with some other school in particular? Depends on that too.\\n\\nAlso, getting a coop in this market (especially for international students) is not as easy as the stats make it sound.\\n\\nI wouldn’t count on getting a coop as a way to make Northeastern affordable tbh because it’s not guaranteed given the market and other factors.\\n\\nGood luck!\\n- Comment 19spbe4g08k03: Fair point, it’s hard to call Northeastern affordable for internationals without funding.\\n\\nOn conversion, Northeastern says more than half of graduates get a full-time offer from a co-op employer. One guide puts it at 52% for master’s students. But these are university numbers and don’t separate international students, whose odds are probably lower because of sponsorship.\\n\\nDo you know how it went for internationals? Did your co-op employer offer a return role and sponsor?\\n- Comment 19sphp0zekj0l: Yes many pay off an entire semesters fees with the coop earnings\\n- Comment 19sq2l6ocn8lg: lol read that carefully OP\\n- Comment 19spqfwqy1tts: I think a lot of international students are hoping the coops would relief their financial stress when choosing NEU, but I don’t think it’s realistic especially for international students to find a coop in this job environment.\\n- Comment 19spb8pwsww8w: Nothing is affordable about studying in northeastern without a grant/scholarship as an international student. Coop makes some money sure but you’re at a temporary entry level position.\\n- Comment 19spd0kxc30w2: I agree with this. Are you trying to compare it with some other school in particular? Depends on that too.\\n\\nAlso, getting a coop in this market (especially for international students) is not as easy as the stats make it sound.\\n\\nI wouldn’t count on getting a coop as a way to make Northeastern affordable tbh because it’s not guaranteed given the market and other factors.\\n\\nGood luck!\\n- Comment 19spbe4g08k03: Fair point, it’s hard to call Northeastern affordable for internationals without funding.\\n\\nOn conversion, Northeastern says more than half of graduates get a full-time offer from a co-op employer. One guide puts it at 52% for master’s students. But these are university numbers and don’t separate international students, whose odds are probably lower because of sponsorship.\\n\\nDo you know how it went for internationals? Did your co-op employer offer a return role and sponsor?\\n- Comment 19sphp0zekj0l: Yes many pay off an entire semesters fees with the coop earnings\\n- Comment 19sq2l6ocn8lg: lol read that carefully OP\\n- Comment 19spqfwqy1tts: I think a lot of international students are hoping the coops would relief their financial stress when choosing NEU, but I don’t think it’s realistic especially for international students to find a coop in this job environment.'}, {'id': '1wwa7d4', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1wwa7d4: NEU students: what was your last UHCS visit actually like? (student project, not a complaint thread)\\n\\n**Matched keywords**: visa\\n**Found in**: post\\n\\n**Quick survey: your honest UHCS experiences (for a class project)**\\n\\nHey all,a few of us are working on a project looking at University Health and Counseling Services and want real student experiences, not just what's on the website. No wrong answers, and feel free to be blunt.\\n\\nIf you've used UHCS (medical or mental health side), we'd love to hear:\\n\\n* What was the actual process like, booking, triage call, walk-in, whatever applied to you?\\n* What was the most frustrating part, if any?\\n* Was there anything that worked *well* that surprised you?\\n* Anything that happened outside normal hours (evenings, weekends) what did you do?\\n* If you have an international/visa status, any experience with medical documentation (RCL, leave of absence, etc.)?\\n* Anything about communication like follow-ups, getting results, knowing what to do next?\\n\\nTotally fine to DM instead of commenting if you'd rather keep it private. Thanks!\"}, {'id': '1xrdctk', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1xrdctk: SMU Cox MSBA vs Northeastern MSBA — which one? Also, thoughts on SMU's new MS in AI for Business?\\n\\n**Matched keywords**: visa\\n**Found in**: post\\n\\nInternational student 3.5 years in payments at a bank. Goal: work in payments (Visa, Mastercard, JPMorgan), ideally in Dallas.\\n\\n**SMU Cox:** admitted to MSBA, partial scholarship, Dallas network\\n\\n**Northeastern (D'Amore-McKim):** admitted to MSBA, scholarship in review, but the co-op looks strong.\\n\\nWhich would you pick for recruiting and sponsorship?   \\nIs Northeastern's co-op worth being in Boston instead of Dallas?\\n\\nAlso, anyone know anything about SMU's new **MS in AI for Business**? Worth it over the MSBA, or too new to tell?  \\nThanks!\"}, {'id': '1xdkyqt', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1xdkyqt: Northeastern for MSF? For fall 2027?\\n\\n**Matched keywords**: visa\\n**Found in**: post\\n\\nWhat do you think about the course and visa situation?'}]\n\nUSER QUERY:\nWhich posts mention visa or CPT rules?\n\nINSTRUCTIONS:\n1. Answer the user's query based ONLY on the information provided in the context.\n2. If the user's query is not related to the context, say \"I don't have enough information to answer this question.\"\n3. If the context doesn't contain enough information to answer the query, say \"I don't have enough information to answer this question.\"\n4. Do not make up information or use knowledge outside of the provided context.\n5. For every piece of evidence you cite, add a reference at the end of the sentence in the format [postID] where postID is the exact ID of the source document.\n6. Example of proper reference: \"Users are experiencing battery life issues with the device [qpmkvr].\"\n7. Make sure all postID references are accurate and correspond to actual post IDs from the provided context.\n8. Do not modify the postID format - keep it exactly as shown in the context (do not add extra characters or modify the ID).\n9. Organize your response using markdown section headers (##) to categorize different perspectives.\n10. Use headers like \"## Positive Perspectives\", \"## Criticisms\", \"## Feature Requests\", \"## Common Issues\", etc. as appropriate for the query.\n11. Under each section header, provide a bulleted list explaining the key points with references.\n\n\nANSWER:\n"